/// True where low <= x <= high.
///
/// Whether each value lies in the closed range from `low` to `high`, both ends included:
/// `low <= x && x <= high` row by row, so the result is a `Column<Bool>` for `where`, `add` and
/// the counts. The comparison is three-valued (section 3.7 of the Language Reference): a row
/// whose value is `NA` is `NA`, and `where` drops it with a note. `low` above `high` is true
/// nowhere.
///
/// x: The column to test.
/// low: The smallest value that counts as inside.
/// high: The largest value that counts as inside.
/// returns: A `Column<Bool>`, `NA` where `x` is.
/// missing values: A row whose value is `NA` is `NA`, neither inside nor outside, and `where` drops it with a note that counts it.
/// coming from: d.where(between(wage, 10, 20)) | subset(d, wage >= 10 & wage <= 20), or dplyr::filter(d, dplyr::between(wage, 10, 20)) | keep if inrange(wage, 10, 20)
/// coming from: All three include both ends. Stata's `inrange` is false for a missing value, so `keep if !inrange(wage, 10, 20)` keeps the rows with no wage; here a missing value is neither inside nor outside, and the note counts it.
/// related: To test whether a value is one of a list, use `is_in`.
/// related: To pull values into a range and not only test them, use `clamp`.
/// related: To keep the rows a test picks, use `where`.
/// see also: is_in, clamp, where
/// example: The rows with a wage from 10 to 20, both ends included; the note counts the rows with no wage.
/// > let wages = dataset(wages)
/// > wages.where(between(wage, 10, 20)).n_rows()
/// 5595
/// note: 211 rows dropped where `between(wage, 10, 20)` was NA; `wages.where(is_missing(wage))` lists them (line 2)
/// example: As a variable: true, false, or `NA` where the value is missing.
/// > wages.add(mid = between(educ, 12, 16)).select([worker, year, educ, mid]).head(3)
/// frame · 3 rows × 4 variables · panel worker × year
///
///       worker   year   educ   mid
///   1        1   2015     17   false
///   2        1   2016     17   false
///   3        1   2017     17   false
/// example: `low` above `high` is true nowhere.
/// > wages.where(between(educ, 16, 12)).n_rows()
/// 0
/// note: no row kept: the result is empty (line 4)
fn between(x: Column<Float>, low: Float, high: Float) -> Column<Bool> {
    low <= x && x <= high
}
